  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A round of showers and some thunderstorms with areas \nof heavy rain could result in some flash flooding risk late Saturday \ninto Sunday.\n\nA front to our south will lift back northward as a warm front later \nSaturday. As this occurs, an incoming shortwave will start to \nincrease the forcing for ascent. It appears that the greatest lift \noccurs Saturday night and Sunday morning, which may become enhanced \nsome by a weak surface low that develops in our vicinity. As a \nresult of the latest timing, most of the area should be \nprecipitation-free during the day Saturday. The precipitable water \nvalues increase from south to north later Saturday and Saturday \nnight to 2.00-2.25 inches, and this will open the door to areas of \nheavier convective rainfall. \n\nWhile the system does look to be progressive, times of excessive \nrainfall rates are certainly possible give the convective nature and \nhigh precipitable water values. The model forecast soundings also \nshow a deep warm cloud layer, which would also increase the \nefficiency of the hourly rainfall rates. Despite the more \nprogressive nature of this system, there continues to be some flash \nflooding risk especially for the urban I-95 corridor where excessive \nrainfall rates will be more prone to rapid runoff. It is the \nurbanized areas and small streams where flash flooding is more \npossible given any rapid runoff. Given the timing of this event, \npeaking overnight Saturday night and Sunday morning, and the model \nforecast soundings showing a deep moist profile, tends to result in \nvery limited instability. As a result, thunder should be rather \nlimited. Given some questions on where the heaviest rainfall amounts \nwill occur and also the progressive nature of this, held off on a \nFlood Watch. \n\nAs a cold front moves across our area on Sunday, drier air will \narrive from the northwest. The main forcing also peels off to our \neast and northeast, bringing an end to the showers/thunder. We may \nhave to wait for a possible secondary cold front to arrive later \nSunday for the main push of dry air, with it then turning noticeably \nless humid in its wake. This secondary front could have some \nconvection tied to it back across the eastern Great Lakes, however \nthese may weaken and/or dissipate by the time they reach our \nnorthwest zones late Sunday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...During the latter half of next week, a slow \nmoving cold front looks to make its way southward across the \narea bringing the potential for showers and storms. \n\nOnce we get through this weekend, attention will turn to Thursday \nand Friday of next week. It should be first noted there is a lot of \nspread in the forecast guidance at this time given this is about a \nweek away. But overall, the signal in guidance is for a slow moving \ncold front to move southward through the area late next Thursday \ninto Friday bring the potential for showers and some storms. PWAT \nvalues along this front look to potentially be quite high (around 2 \ninches) but there remains some questions about how much instability \nthere will be as well as the speed and timing of the front as it \nmoves through. The upshot of all this is that while forecast \nconfidence is low, this period will need to be monitored for heavy \nrain potential.",
